The Espressif `esp-tflite-micro` component is a practical starting point in ESP-IDF. The official examples teach different jobs:
• `hello_world` demonstrates model input, output, and a minimal inference loop;
• `micro_speech` recognizes a small set of words or commands from microphone audio windows;
• `person_detection` classifies whether a person appears in a camera frame on supported hardware.

Examples have board and peripheral requirements; person detection, for example, may need a camera and a supported ESP32-S3 board. Do not assume one Arduino IDE library works with every ESP32: verify its version, compiler/core compatibility, model, and supported operators. For a first project, start from the official ESP-IDF example, pin its dependency version, and build for the exact board.

A `MicroInterpreter` runs the model; `MicroMutableOpResolver` should register only operations the model uses. Check `AllocateTensors()`, match input shape/type and quantization parameters to the model, and check the result of `Invoke()`. Unneeded operators can increase flash and RAM use.
